 "introductionText": "1. What FRUS answers directly. The phrase \"soft power\" never appears in FRUS; officials of 1949–1977 wrote instead of the \"information program\", \"psychological strategy\", the \"image\" of the United States and its \"prestige\". In that vocabulary, about 1,400 documents in the 81 East Asian and public-diplomacy volumes discuss the effort, and they show a consistent assessment: the information program could win attention among educated elites but rarely moved opinion against what American policy itself was doing. Ambassador at Large Jessup's 1949 review proposed that \"A considerably expanded information program both in Communist and non-Communist areas of Asia is called for\", to align the United States with Asian nationalism (Foreign Relations of the United States, 1949, The Far East and Australasia, Volume VII, Part 2, Document 386, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1949v07p2/d386); the National Security Council's 1957 report on the program conceded that \"the trend toward accommodation to Communist pressures in the area continued\" (Foreign Relations of the United States, 1955–1957, Volume IX, Document 207, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1955-57v09/d207); Ambassador Reischauer in 1965 judged that Embassy and information-service work in Japan had \"met with some success\" intellectually while \"the basic emotional response remains unchanged\" (Foreign Relations of the United States, 1964–1968, Volume XXIX, Part 2, Japan, Document 46, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1964-68v29p2/d46); and the Information Agency's area office reported in 1969 that the American image in Asia \"depended more on the state of bi-lateral relations … than on developments within the US\" (Foreign Relations of the United States, 1917–1972, Volume VIII, Public Diplomacy, 1969–1972, Document 31, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1917-72PubDipv08/d31). Twenty-three documents carry the argument below.\n\n2. Where FRUS points for the rest. *Where the printed documents came from:* more than half of the 1,400 come from the State Department's own files (Record Group 59), chiefly the decimal central files and, for policy papers, the Executive Secretariat's \"Records Relating to National Security Council Policy Papers\" (Record Group 59, entry A1 1586E, National Archives identifier 2839192, 1947–1979, partly restricted under national-security exemptions, College Park), which also holds the annual reports on the information program; and about one in eight come from the Information Agency's own records (Record Group 306, the Records of the U.S. Information Agency, identifier 622) — the Director's subject files and the East Asia area office's files, which the bundled finding aids can only trace to an accession that NARA has since split across 3 to 18 series each, so no single shelf can be named; the presidential libraries' National Security Files supply most of the rest. *What the editors cited without printing:* the same National Security Council policy-papers series (its lots 63 D 351 and 66 D 95); the Executive Secretariat's \"Subject and Special Files\" (Record Group 59, entry A1 1586C, identifier 2839191, 1953–1961, partly restricted); the Policy Planning Council's \"Records Relating to Activities with the National Security Council\" (Record Group 59, entries A1 1583E–F, identifier 2838992, 1948–1961, partly restricted); and, cited behind the 1957 report, the Information Agency's Office of Policy and Plans \"Subject Files\" (Record Group 306, entries UD-WW 151 to 382, identifier 1257679, 1953–1971, 47 linear feet, partly restricted), the closest shelf to the program's own policy guidance; the agency's \"Alphabetical Subject Files Containing Policy Guidance Records\" (Record Group 306, entry P 311, identifier 40943515, 1953–1969, partly restricted) is named in the volumes' source lists. Only about one document in four of the 1,400 carries such a citation, so this second list is thin.\n\n3. What this request cannot get from FRUS. Three things. First, the term itself: a search for \"soft power\" across all 553 volumes found nothing, against a control (\"Department of State\") found in every volume of the scope and an impossible test word found nowhere; the question has to be asked in the period's own words. Second, measured effectiveness: the posts' surveys, the Information Agency's opinion research and the country plans are summarized but not printed — the 1969 Manila conference report, sixty pages, survives in FRUS only as a summary, and the 1975 post cables are printed only as abstracts. Those are in the Information Agency's records (Record Group 306), where the bundled finding aids can name the record group and accessions but not one series. Third, the full round of 1952 embassy reports on information-program effectiveness: a search for the Department's 1952 circular asking every major post to assess its program found one printed reply, from Saigon; the rest sit in the State Department's 1950–54 decimal file under class 511 (the class the 1950s filing manual labels \"International Informational and Educational Relations, Cultural Affairs and Programs\").",
